# The project manifest (§5.2, §6.1)

This module is `lit.toml`, the project manifest. The prose is the specification.
The chunks after it are the implementation. Every table is optional, so an absent
or empty file is valid — the CLI then falls back to its argument-derived defaults.

## What the manifest declares {#sec:TLsr}

A `[[document]]` table names the document: its id, title, authors, the `.nw` files
in order, and the prose dialect. A `[tangle]` table sets the default output
directory, and a `[[tangle.root]]` table per root sets that root's line-pragma
format. A `[weave]` table holds the highlight language keys (§5.5).

```toml
[[document]]
id = "lit"
title = "lit: a literate programming system"
files = ["intro.nw", "parser.nw", "weave.nw"]
dialect = "markdown"   # or "html"; default "markdown" (§6.1)

[tangle]
outdir = "."

[[tangle.root]]
chunk = "server/app.py"
line-pragma = '# %L "%F"%N'
```

## Root chunks and the line pragma {#sec:E4N0}

A chunk whose name is path-shaped — it contains a `/`, or it matches a file
extension — is a root. `notangle -L` takes a printf-ish format for its line
pragma, and the correct format is language-specific: its own default is C's
`#line`. So a single boolean would be wrong — it would emit `#line` into Python
and Rust. lit takes a per-root format string instead, and it defaults to *off*.

There is a verified caveat. The `-L` flag disables indentation propagation, so an
inner chunk's lines land at column 0, and it splits the code around a chunk use
onto separate lines with column-preserving padding. So pragma'd output is a
different shape, not just annotated text. This is acceptable for generated code,
and it is why the default is off.

## Two prose dialects {#sec:ZZAp}

A docs chunk is authored in one of two dialects, set per document by `dialect`.
The `markdown` dialect (the default) is CommonMark: paragraphs, emphasis, lists,
links, headings, and backtick code all become HTML, and the fenced float
directives are numbered floats (§6.4). The `html` dialect is raw HTML — the escape
hatch, and what any construct the Markdown directives do not cover (for example a
`<aside class="sidenote">`) falls back to as a raw-HTML island.

Both dialects share one pipeline (§6.2). Markdown is a post-`markup` transform on
the already-reassembled prose. Everything downstream — the numbering, the refs,
the math render, the sidenote hoist, `site.json` — is dialect-agnostic and
byte-identical.

## How the code implements this {#sec:Er9s}

This module is a set of serde structs, one per manifest table, plus one `impl`
block that loads the file and answers the questions the rest of lit asks of it:
which document, and which tangle roots carry a line-pragma format. The file is
laid out the way a reader meets it: the module banner, the imports, the error
type, then each table struct in source order, then the loader and its queries,
then the tests.
